WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:06.010
Last episode's problem was drift:

2
00:00:06.010 --> 00:00:09.680
the same policy, written twice, in two tech stacks.

3
00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:12.520
Chenile's answer is almost annoyingly simple.

4
00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:14.720
There's only one kind of policy.

5
00:00:15.370 --> 00:00:17.860
A policy is a Chenile interceptor.

6
00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:23.660
The exact same interceptor contract runs inside the auth gateway from chenile-security,

7
00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:27.450
and inside every service's pipeline, at the last mile.

8
00:00:27.550 --> 00:00:29.030
You don't write two versions.

9
00:00:29.030 --> 00:00:29.690
You write one

10
00:00:29.690 --> 00:00:31.320
— and choose where it runs.

11
00:00:31.970 --> 00:00:34.490
At the last mile, attach an authorization

12
00:00:34.490 --> 00:00:37.570
policy to the operation with an extension key.

13
00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:39.250
Call the service directly:

14
00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:42.100
the policy runs with full resource context.

15
00:00:42.750 --> 00:00:45.860
At the edge, bring up the Chenile gateway starter.

16
00:00:45.860 --> 00:00:48.600
It validates the token, resolves the tenant,

17
00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:51.470
and relays trusted claims to the services.

18
00:00:51.570 --> 00:00:53.340
An unauthenticated request?

19
00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:53.870
Rejected

20
00:00:53.870 --> 00:00:56.410
— before it ever reaches a service.

21
00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:57.840
Now run both.

22
00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:00.830
A valid user asks for an order they don't own.

23
00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:02.290
The edge lets them in.

24
00:01:02.290 --> 00:01:03.770
The last mile blocks it.

25
00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:04.760
Defence in depth

26
00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:06.490
— with one policy model.

27
00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:10.460
Moving a policy from the last mile to the gateway,

28
00:01:10.460 --> 00:01:13.320
or running it in both, is a registry change

29
00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:14.450
— not a refactor.

30
00:01:14.450 --> 00:01:16.500
Placement is configuration.

31
00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:18.710
And it's not just HTTP.

32
00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:21.630
With chenile-proxies and chenile-messaging,

33
00:01:21.630 --> 00:01:27.110
the same policy guards an internal interface call, and a message off a queue.

34
00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:29.090
Write a policy once.

35
00:01:29.090 --> 00:01:32.070
Run it at the gateway, the last mile, or both

36
00:01:32.070 --> 00:01:34.050
— chosen by configuration.

37
00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:35.530
Next episode:

38
00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:37.120
test once, run everywhere

39
00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:39.790
— behaviour-driven testing in Chenile.
